# Every text file is checked out with LF line endings, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites text files
# to CRLF on checkout. This tree cannot take that: the invariant suite scans
# the source byte for byte, the JSON fixtures are "never edited" and replayed
# as recorded, and trybuild compares compiler output against checked-in
# `.stderr` files. A CRLF checkout changes the bytes every one of those reads,
# and the board would go red on Windows for a reason that is not in any
# commit. `text=auto` leaves alone what git detects as binary, and git detects
# by content rather than by name -- which is why the `.bin` files are named.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Every `.bin` file, never converted in either direction: the fixture corpus
# and the keystore snapshots in `crates/mochimo-crypto/testdata/`. Detection
# is not enough for them, because two are printable text by content:
# `M-requests.bin` holds request records as JSON lines, and
# `C-addr-from-wots-fill42_in.bin` is 2,144 bytes of 0x42 -- `git ls-files
# --eol -- '*.bin'` prints them as `i/lf` and `i/none`. Neither holds a CR, so
# a checkout leaves their bytes as they are; but a `.bin` git reads as text
# and that holds a CR LF pair would have the pair rewritten by `git add`, and
# its bytes are the thing it exists to hold. Named, the rule no longer depends
# on what the next one happens to contain.
*.bin -text

# Vendored standards texts whose bytes are pinned by hash: never converted at
# all, in either direction.
docs/standards/*.txt -text
